{"module":"twenty-twenties","voice":"male","duration":287.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The 2020s: reckoning and backlash","start":0.0,"end":2.6,"words":[[0.05,0.18],[0.18,1.09],[1.32,1.78],[1.78,1.97],[1.97,2.36]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Many people believe the racial history in these modules is over.","start":3.4,"end":7.49,"words":[[3.44,3.73],[3.73,4.09],[4.09,4.46],[4.46,4.6],[4.6,5.04],[5.04,5.55],[5.55,5.69],[5.69,5.91],[5.91,6.42],[6.42,6.57],[6.57,6.93]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The 2020s show both how much it still shapes daily life and how quickly public attention can turn.","start":7.84,"end":14.19,"words":[[7.89,8.03],[8.03,8.96],[8.96,9.18],[9.18,9.46],[9.46,9.68],[9.68,9.97],[9.97,10.11],[10.11,10.4],[10.4,10.76],[10.76,11.11],[11.11,11.4],[11.4,11.62],[11.62,11.83],[11.83,12.26],[12.26,12.69],[12.69,13.19],[13.19,13.41],[13.41,13.69]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Several of these cases began with a call to police or a neighbor's suspicion.","start":14.54,"end":19.3,"words":[[14.58,15.03],[15.03,15.18],[15.18,15.4],[15.4,15.85],[15.85,16.23],[16.23,16.45],[16.45,16.6],[16.6,16.83],[16.83,16.98],[16.98,17.35],[17.35,17.43],[17.43,17.58],[17.58,18.03],[18.03,18.7]]},{"el":"h0","text":"2020","start":19.65,"end":20.58,"words":[[19.7,20.42]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Ahmaud Arbery was jogging in Brunswick, Georgia, in February 2020 when three white men decided he looked suspicious, chased him in pickup trucks and shot him.","start":21.28,"end":31.81,"words":[[21.32,21.61],[21.61,21.98],[21.98,22.19],[22.19,22.63],[22.63,22.78],[22.78,23.44],[23.69,24.21],[24.46,24.61],[24.61,25.19],[25.19,26.07],[26.07,26.29],[26.29,26.51],[26.51,26.8],[26.8,27.02],[27.02,27.61],[27.61,27.75],[27.75,28.05],[28.05,28.7],[28.96,29.4],[29.4,29.62],[29.62,29.76],[29.76,30.13],[30.13,30.5],[30.5,30.72],[30.72,30.94],[30.94,31.23]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Local prosecutors declined to charge them until a video surfaced.","start":32.16,"end":36.62,"words":[[32.21,32.62],[32.62,33.39],[33.39,33.94],[33.94,34.08],[34.08,34.43],[34.43,34.63],[34.63,34.98],[34.98,35.12],[35.12,35.61],[35.61,36.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"All three were convicted of murder in 2021 and of federal hate crimes in 2022, and Georgia repealed its Civil War-era citizen's arrest law.","start":36.97,"end":47.6,"words":[[37.02,37.17],[37.17,37.41],[37.41,37.57],[37.57,38.28],[38.28,38.44],[38.44,38.75],[38.75,38.91],[38.91,40.09],[40.09,40.33],[40.33,40.49],[40.49,41.04],[41.04,41.36],[41.36,41.83],[41.83,41.99],[41.99,43.17],[43.45,43.68],[43.68,44.16],[44.16,44.63],[44.63,44.87],[44.87,45.26],[45.26,45.81],[45.81,46.45],[46.45,46.84],[46.84,47.08]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In March, Louisville police serving a no-knock warrant shot and killed Breonna Taylor in her apartment; the city then banned no-knock warrants.","start":47.95,"end":57.21,"words":[[48.0,48.16],[48.16,48.57],[48.85,49.42],[49.42,49.83],[49.83,50.24],[50.24,50.4],[50.4,50.89],[50.89,51.38],[51.38,51.62],[51.62,51.86],[51.86,52.19],[52.19,52.76],[52.76,53.16],[53.16,53.33],[53.33,53.49],[53.49,54.22],[54.51,54.67],[54.67,54.99],[54.99,55.24],[55.24,55.56],[55.56,56.05],[56.05,56.7]]},{"el":"s0","text":"On May 25, a store clerk called 911 about a suspected counterfeit $20 bill, and Minneapolis officer Derek Chauvin knelt on George Floyd's neck for more than nine minutes.","start":57.56,"end":71.07,"words":[[57.61,57.77],[57.77,58.01],[58.01,58.88],[59.15,59.31],[59.31,59.55],[59.55,59.86],[59.86,60.18],[60.18,61.68],[61.68,62.07],[62.07,62.23],[62.23,62.94],[62.94,63.73],[63.73,64.52],[64.52,64.84],[65.11,65.35],[65.35,66.22],[66.22,66.61],[66.61,67.01],[67.01,67.48],[67.48,67.8],[67.8,67.95],[67.95,68.35],[68.35,68.82],[68.82,69.06],[69.06,69.22],[69.22,69.37],[69.37,69.61],[69.61,69.93],[69.93,70.48]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Chauvin was convicted of murder in 2021.","start":71.42,"end":74.94,"words":[[71.48,71.9],[71.9,72.11],[72.11,72.75],[72.75,72.89],[72.89,73.17],[73.17,73.31],[73.31,74.44]]},{"el":"h1","text":"The largest protests in U.S. history","start":75.84,"end":78.4,"words":[[75.88,76.01],[76.01,76.45],[76.45,77.02],[77.02,77.15],[77.15,77.46],[77.78,78.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Surveys estimated that 15 million to 26 million Americans took part in protests in the weeks after Floyd's murder, in small towns as well as cities.","start":79.1,"end":88.7,"words":[[79.14,79.57],[79.57,80.29],[80.29,80.5],[80.5,80.93],[80.93,81.36],[81.36,81.51],[81.51,82.22],[82.22,82.65],[82.65,83.3],[83.3,83.52],[83.52,83.73],[83.73,83.87],[83.87,84.52],[84.52,84.66],[84.66,84.81],[84.81,85.09],[85.09,85.38],[85.38,85.81],[85.81,86.17],[86.42,86.56],[86.56,86.85],[86.85,87.21],[87.21,87.35],[87.35,87.57],[87.57,87.71],[87.71,88.14]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The House of Representatives passed the George Floyd Justice in Policing Act in 2020 and 2021; it never passed the Senate.","start":89.05,"end":97.93,"words":[[89.1,89.25],[89.25,89.55],[89.55,89.7],[89.7,90.73],[90.73,91.03],[91.03,91.18],[91.18,91.55],[91.55,91.92],[91.92,92.44],[92.44,92.59],[92.59,93.11],[93.11,93.33],[93.33,93.48],[93.48,94.37],[94.37,94.59],[94.59,95.78],[96.04,96.19],[96.19,96.48],[96.48,96.78],[96.78,96.93],[96.93,97.37]]},{"el":"s1","text":"A 2022 executive order set policing standards for federal agencies; it was revoked in 2025.","start":98.28,"end":106.4,"words":[[98.32,98.47],[98.47,99.51],[99.51,100.25],[100.25,100.47],[100.47,100.7],[100.7,101.22],[101.22,101.81],[101.81,101.96],[101.96,102.48],[102.48,103.22],[103.48,103.63],[103.63,103.85],[103.85,104.37],[104.37,104.51],[104.51,105.78]]},{"el":"s1","text":"New York repealed a law that had kept police disciplinary records secret and made it a civil rights violation to call police on someone falsely because of their race.","start":106.75,"end":117.14,"words":[[106.8,107.03],[107.03,107.27],[107.27,107.74],[107.74,107.9],[107.9,108.06],[108.06,108.29],[108.29,108.53],[108.53,108.84],[108.84,109.24],[109.24,110.1],[110.1,110.57],[110.57,111.04],[111.04,111.28],[111.28,111.59],[111.59,111.75],[111.75,111.91],[111.91,112.3],[112.3,112.69],[112.69,113.48],[113.48,113.64],[113.64,113.87],[113.87,114.26],[114.26,114.42],[114.42,114.89],[114.89,115.36],[115.36,115.76],[115.76,115.91],[115.91,116.15],[116.15,116.54]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Did the changes work?","start":118.04,"end":119.51,"words":[[118.12,118.29],[118.29,118.41],[118.41,118.93],[118.93,119.16]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Mapping Police Violence recorded more police killings in 2023 than in any year since it began tracking in 2013.","start":120.21,"end":128.34,"words":[[120.26,120.65],[120.65,121.04],[121.04,121.66],[121.66,122.2],[122.2,122.36],[122.36,122.74],[122.74,123.21],[123.21,123.36],[123.36,124.53],[124.53,124.76],[124.76,124.91],[124.91,125.15],[125.15,125.38],[125.38,125.69],[125.69,125.84],[125.84,126.23],[126.23,126.69],[126.69,126.85],[126.85,127.78]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Murders rose about 30% in 2020, the largest one-year increase on record, then fell sharply by 2024.","start":128.69,"end":138.17,"words":[[128.76,129.19],[129.19,129.52],[129.52,129.95],[129.95,130.8],[130.8,130.97],[130.97,132.07],[132.36,132.53],[132.53,133.13],[133.13,133.64],[133.64,134.14],[134.14,134.31],[134.31,134.82],[135.12,135.37],[135.37,135.63],[135.63,136.05],[136.05,136.31],[136.31,137.58]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Researchers still debate how much the pandemic, gun sales, changes in policing and the protests themselves contributed.","start":138.52,"end":146.55,"words":[[138.56,139.13],[139.13,139.41],[139.41,139.84],[139.84,140.06],[140.06,140.34],[140.34,140.49],[140.49,141.13],[141.38,141.6],[141.6,142.03],[142.28,142.92],[142.92,143.06],[143.06,143.56],[143.56,143.78],[143.78,143.92],[143.92,144.57],[144.57,145.14],[145.14,146.07]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Debates over “defunding” or reforming police split cities, some of which cut police budgets and then restored them.","start":146.9,"end":154.04,"words":[[146.94,147.47],[147.47,147.77],[147.77,148.37],[148.37,148.44],[148.44,148.97],[148.97,149.34],[149.34,149.72],[149.72,150.17],[150.43,150.66],[150.66,150.81],[150.81,151.11],[151.11,151.33],[151.33,151.71],[151.71,152.24],[152.24,152.46],[152.46,152.69],[152.69,153.14],[153.14,153.44]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Calls and suspicion that still turn deadly","start":154.94,"end":158.04,"words":[[154.98,155.36],[155.36,155.65],[155.65,156.41],[156.41,156.69],[156.69,157.07],[157.07,157.36],[157.36,157.83]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In January 2023, Memphis officers from a special unit beat Tyre Nichols after a traffic stop; he died three days later, and the unit was disbanded.","start":158.74,"end":169.8,"words":[[158.79,158.94],[158.94,159.62],[159.62,160.83],[161.09,161.55],[161.55,162.0],[162.0,162.3],[162.3,162.45],[162.45,162.9],[162.9,163.28],[163.28,163.51],[163.51,163.81],[163.81,164.26],[164.26,164.57],[164.57,164.72],[164.72,165.17],[165.17,165.55],[165.81,165.96],[165.96,166.26],[166.26,166.49],[166.49,166.79],[166.79,167.25],[167.51,167.74],[167.74,167.89],[167.89,168.27],[168.27,168.49],[168.49,169.25]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In April 2023, 16-year-old Ralph Yarl was shot in Kansas City after ringing the wrong doorbell to pick up his brothers; he survived.","start":170.15,"end":179.06,"words":[[170.2,170.35],[170.35,170.77],[170.77,171.91],[172.16,173.22],[173.22,173.5],[173.5,173.72],[173.72,173.93],[173.93,174.14],[174.14,174.29],[174.29,174.71],[174.71,175.0],[175.0,175.28],[175.28,175.63],[175.63,175.78],[175.78,175.99],[175.99,176.34],[176.34,176.49],[176.49,176.7],[176.7,176.84],[176.84,177.05],[177.05,177.55],[177.8,177.94],[177.94,178.51]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In June 2023, Ajike Owens was shot through a neighbor's door in Florida after a dispute over children playing; the neighbor was convicted of manslaughter.","start":179.41,"end":189.17,"words":[[179.46,179.6],[179.6,179.88],[179.88,181.01],[181.25,181.67],[181.67,182.03],[182.03,182.24],[182.24,182.45],[182.45,182.66],[182.66,182.8],[182.8,183.22],[183.22,183.36],[183.36,183.5],[183.5,184.0],[184.0,184.28],[184.28,184.42],[184.42,184.91],[184.91,185.19],[185.19,185.76],[185.76,186.25],[186.49,186.64],[186.64,186.99],[186.99,187.2],[187.2,187.83],[187.83,187.97],[187.97,188.61]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In July 2024, Sonya Massey called 911 for help about a possible prowler, and an Illinois deputy who responded shot her in her own kitchen; he was convicted of second-degree murder in 2025.","start":189.52,"end":203.28,"words":[[189.56,189.7],[189.7,190.12],[190.12,191.16],[191.4,191.75],[191.75,192.02],[192.02,192.3],[192.3,193.62],[193.62,193.76],[193.76,194.03],[194.03,194.38],[194.38,194.52],[194.52,195.0],[195.0,195.49],[195.73,195.94],[195.94,196.08],[196.08,196.49],[196.49,196.98],[196.98,197.12],[197.12,197.74],[197.74,197.95],[197.95,198.09],[198.09,198.23],[198.23,198.36],[198.36,198.57],[198.57,199.06],[199.3,199.44],[199.44,199.65],[199.65,200.27],[200.27,200.41],[200.41,201.17],[201.17,201.45],[201.45,201.59],[201.59,202.77]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The contested deaths page tracks disputed hanging deaths that continue today.","start":203.63,"end":208.54,"words":[[203.68,203.82],[203.82,204.46],[204.46,204.75],[204.75,205.1],[205.1,205.46],[205.46,206.1],[206.1,206.46],[206.46,206.74],[206.74,206.96],[206.96,207.53],[207.53,207.95]]},{"el":"h4","text":"The backlash","start":209.44,"end":210.44,"words":[[209.49,209.67],[209.67,210.2]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Since 2021, more than a dozen states have passed laws or policies restricting how teachers can discuss race and racism.","start":211.14,"end":219.79,"words":[[211.18,211.49],[211.49,212.72],[212.99,213.14],[213.14,213.37],[213.37,213.53],[213.53,213.91],[213.91,214.37],[214.37,214.61],[214.61,214.91],[214.91,215.14],[215.14,215.22],[215.22,215.76],[215.76,216.53],[216.53,216.76],[216.76,217.22],[217.22,217.45],[217.45,217.91],[217.91,218.22],[218.22,218.45],[218.45,219.15]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Florida rejected a pilot AP African American Studies course in 2023.","start":220.14,"end":226.17,"words":[[220.19,220.74],[220.74,221.44],[221.44,221.6],[221.6,222.07],[222.07,222.22],[222.22,222.77],[222.77,223.39],[223.39,223.86],[223.86,224.1],[224.1,224.25],[224.25,225.51]]},{"el":"s4","text":"That June, the Supreme Court ended race-conscious college admissions in Students for Fair Admissions v. Harvard.","start":226.52,"end":235.59,"words":[[226.56,226.85],[226.85,227.31],[227.64,227.83],[227.83,228.39],[228.39,228.67],[228.67,229.14],[229.14,230.08],[230.08,230.64],[230.64,231.39],[231.39,231.58],[231.58,232.42],[232.42,232.61],[232.61,232.89],[232.89,233.64],[233.64,233.92],[234.38,234.95]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In January 2025, executive orders ended federal diversity, equity and inclusion programs, and many companies scaled back their own.","start":235.94,"end":246.01,"words":[[235.99,236.14],[236.14,236.78],[236.78,238.0],[238.25,238.96],[238.96,239.25],[239.25,239.6],[239.6,240.1],[240.1,240.82],[241.07,241.5],[241.5,241.71],[241.71,242.28],[242.28,243.0],[243.25,243.46],[243.46,243.75],[243.75,244.32],[244.32,244.75],[244.75,244.96],[244.96,245.18],[245.18,245.46]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Supporters call these changes a return to colorblind rules; opponents say they erase history and remove tools for addressing discrimination that still exists.","start":246.36,"end":256.95,"words":[[246.41,246.96],[246.96,247.2],[247.2,247.44],[247.44,248.15],[248.15,248.31],[248.31,248.7],[248.7,248.86],[248.86,249.65],[249.65,250.05],[250.33,251.04],[251.04,251.28],[251.28,251.51],[251.51,251.91],[251.91,252.46],[252.46,252.7],[252.7,253.1],[253.1,253.41],[253.41,253.57],[253.57,254.13],[254.13,255.23],[255.23,255.47],[255.47,255.79],[255.79,256.42]]},{"el":"h5","text":"What's moving now","start":257.85,"end":259.09,"words":[[257.9,258.26],[258.26,258.72],[258.72,259.0]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The CROWN Act, which bans discrimination based on natural hair texture and protective styles such as braids and locs, has passed in more than 20 states since California's in 2019; the House passed a federal version in 2022.","start":259.79,"end":275.49,"words":[[259.83,259.98],[259.98,260.35],[260.35,260.65],[260.91,261.21],[261.21,261.5],[261.5,262.54],[262.54,262.91],[262.91,263.06],[263.06,263.66],[263.66,263.88],[263.88,264.4],[264.4,264.62],[264.62,265.29],[265.29,265.74],[265.74,266.04],[266.04,266.18],[266.18,266.63],[266.63,266.85],[266.85,267.22],[267.48,267.71],[267.71,268.01],[268.01,268.15],[268.15,268.3],[268.3,268.53],[268.53,268.97],[268.97,269.42],[269.42,269.71],[269.71,270.46],[270.46,270.61],[270.61,271.65],[271.91,272.06],[272.06,272.35],[272.35,272.65],[272.65,272.8],[272.8,273.32],[273.32,273.69],[273.69,273.84],[273.84,274.95]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Cities from Evanston to Tulsa are experimenting with repair.","start":275.84,"end":279.96,"words":[[275.88,276.26],[276.26,276.57],[276.57,277.18],[277.18,277.34],[277.34,277.72],[277.72,277.8],[277.8,278.79],[278.79,279.02],[279.02,279.48]]},{"el":"s5","text":"And CAREN-style laws are exactly the kind of local and state win this campaign is built to spread.","start":280.31,"end":286.38,"words":[[280.35,280.57],[280.57,281.31],[281.31,281.53],[281.53,281.6],[281.6,282.19],[282.19,282.34],[282.34,282.71],[282.71,282.86],[282.86,283.3],[283.3,283.52],[283.52,283.89],[283.89,284.11],[284.11,284.34],[284.34,284.85],[284.85,285.0],[285.0,285.3],[285.3,285.44],[285.44,285.89]]}]}